[agent] **Final contents: exactly one row per `row_id`, each the latest revision.** Verified — the platform's offline read (via the Feature Query Service) returns 400 rows / 400 distinct `row_id`s, and all 400 `(row_id, updated_at)` pairs match the true max-`updated_at` per key computed from the raw batches. Online lookups return the same latest values.
[agent] ### A note on what the interface required
[agent] Naively loading the three batches (490 rows, 400 distinct keys) did **not** produce the correct result, and this is worth flagging:
[agent] - The **offline store is Delta append-only** — inserting the batches left all 490 revisions (duplicates per key); it does not upsert/precombine on the event-time column. The CLI `fg create` exposes no time-travel-format option (e.g. Hudi) to get offline upsert-by-key behavior.
[agent] - The **online store resolved duplicate keys by arrival order, not event-time** — across separately-inserted batches it was effectively nondeterministic (e.g. R00001 kept the latest, but R00003/R00006 kept older revisions). Registering `updated_at` as the event-time column did **not** make either store pick the latest revision across batches.
[agent] To produce the correct deliverable I kept the dedup **on the platform**: ran a Trino window-function query (`ROW_NUMBER() … PARTITION BY row_id ORDER BY updated_at DESC`) over the loaded offline data to select the latest revision per key, then recreated the table and loaded that single deduplicated 400-row set. With one revision per key in a single commit, both stores are now correct and deterministic.
[agent] The capability gap, if useful for improving the interface: **`hops fg insert` has no event-time-based upsert/precombine semantics** (and `fg create` no Hudi/upsert table-format flag), so "latest revision wins" across out-of-order batches isn't achievable by loading the batches directly — it requires a manual platform-side dedup-and-reload.
